    <DataNode elementId="CPD-3631" textLabel="(-)-maackiain" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="HUKSJTUUSUGIDC-ZBEGNZNMSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="210" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="chawkins, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: Maackiain Maackiain| is a common pterocarpan that has been detected in many legumes |CITS: [5677639][24221677][14723332][15935275][25819294][26960774]|. Within the two subtribes &lt;i&gt;Diocleinae&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;Erythrininae&lt;/i&gt; it was found in 12 species representing 4 genera |CITS: [Ingham90]|. Maackiain was originally discovered in the |FRAME: TAX-37501| tree |CITS: [13918216]|, which is named after its discoverer, the 19th-century Siberian explorer Karlovich Maack. Maackiain can be found in both a (-) and a (+) conformations. In chickpea, where it is a major pterocarpan phytoalexin together with medicarpin, it occurs exclusively in the (-) [(6aR,11aR)} conformation |CITS: [8071365]|. |FRAME: CPD-3631 &quot;(-)-Maackiain&quot;| was reported to possess antiallergic properties, alleviating nasal symptoms through the inhibition of H1R (histamine H1 receptor) and IL-4 (interleukin-4) gene expression |CITS: [26516579]|.</Comment>
